{"duration_api_ms":45251,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"941c5b97-ab90-4a4d-ad94-715d1832b2e2","total_cost_usd":0.1953764,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12919,"cache_read_input_tokens":93222,"output_tokens":3667,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1064},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12919,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2052,"cache_read_input_tokens":23923,"cache_creation_input_tokens":389,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":389},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":10,"outputTokens":3667,"cacheReadInputTokens":93222,"cacheCreationInputTokens":12919,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.1953764,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":1064,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":6,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"```json\n{\"verdict\":\"reject\",\"diff_digest\":\"50d5c160c29eedf8cec2589df5af96edb6e70ae2b3ea3ebe52dbc0ac2b988d80\",\"violations\":[{\"type\":16,\"location\":\"cc/silo/transaction.cc:55 (EVOLVE-BLOCK silo-lock-order-policy, order_priority の return 行; パッチ適用後の行番号)\",\"correctness_impact\":\"The priority function reads e.locked and puts it in bit 63: (static_cast<uint64_t>(e.locked) << 63u). Rule R8 says the priority is computed only from the snapshot's epoch and tid and must not use locked. This breaks that read contract. e.locked is a racy copy of another transaction's lock bit, read once by the skeleton before sorting. With bit 63 set, the skeleton's descending sort always moves elements that were locked at read time to the front. So the lock order now depends on concurrent lock state that the rules exclude, not on epoch and tid. This does not directly break serializability, because locking is still no-wait with abort on conflict (R6), and validation still runs after all locks are taken (R7). But the mechanism specification (digest 4566966a…) and the model-check result both assume R8 holds. This candidate is outside that registered contract, so the 'no counterexample' result cannot be inherited. The helper signature, the calls, and the three no-op functions are unchanged, and the diff stays between the markers.\",\"verifier_blind_spot\":\"Lock order and the priority inputs are not written to the trace. The DSG/G2 verifier only sees committed transactions' C/R/W lines, so it cannot see which snapshot field the priority function read. Reading locked also does not change serializability, so verify stays green. The effect shows up only as a liveness pattern in aborted transactions: early lock conflicts caused by locking already-locked elements first. Commit-path traces never emit that. The model check was also run with R8 assumed, so it does not reach this candidate either. Only a static review can catch this.\"}],\"nits\":[{\"finding\":\"order_enabled always returns true, so the policy covers every UPDATE-only write set. That alone is within the rules; this is only a note that the scope is wide.\"},{\"finding\":\"Epoch is packed with << 29u. That matches tuple.hh's 29-bit tid and 32-bit epoch (bits 29..60), so there is no overlap. It does depend on the skeleton's field widths staying as they are. If only epoch and tid are used, the value is effectively the version order, which is close to the stock key order, so any performance gain is unclear. Non-blocking.\"},{\"finding\":\"An empty line was added before #else. It is inside the markers and does not matter.\"}],\"proposed_tests\":[{\"mutation\":\"Add a static contract check for R8 on the order_priority body of any silo-lock-order-policy candidate. The only allowed reads are e.epoch and e.tid. Positive control: use this candidate's '(static_cast<uint64_t>(e.locked) << 63u)' as the mutation, and confirm the check turns red on it. Negative control: the same body with that term removed, which should pass.\",\"expected_gate\":\"reject (read contract violation; R8)\",\"machine_judgment\":\"On the diff lines of the EVOLVE-BLOCK, extract member accesses on the EntryContext parameter of order_priority (regex on e\\\\.(\\\\w+), or a clang AST MemberExpr). If any field other than {epoch, tid} appears, reject. The driver confirms two things: the mutation input (this candidate) is red, and the input with the term removed is green, so the check cannot be always-true. Both outcomes are saved as mutation_red_gate records.\"},{\"mutation\":\"Skeleton-side guard (a proposal, needs human review): change the read_word lambda in the patch's validationPhase so it always passes locked as false to the candidate, or remove the locked field from EntryContext. Positive control: with the skeleton temporarily reverted, a unit test with an order_priority that depends on locked (returns 1 if locked, else 0) must see different values for a locked and an unlocked element.\",\"expected_gate\":\"indeterminate (a change to the skeleton itself; needs human review)\",\"machine_judgment\":\"In a single-TU unit test, create a write set with one locked and one unlocked tidword, run sort_write_set, and check that the resulting order does not depend on locked. On the mutation (skeleton that passes locked through), the order must change and the test must go red.\"}],\"uncertainty\":\"The gallery I was given only defines types 1–26; I don't have definitions for types 27–30. I classified the R8 violation as type 16, the closest match (reading outside the allowed inputs / polluting the policy's inputs). If one of types 27–30 is a dedicated 'mechanism spec rule violation' type, that type is the better label. I did not claim that using locked breaks serializability. With no-wait (R6), validation after all locks (R7), and the order being a permutation (R5), I expect correctness holds for any lock order. The reject is based on violating the explicitly stated mechanism rule R8, and on the model-check guarantee not carrying over to this candidate. The external/ccbench transaction.cc in this worktree does not have the patch applied, so I checked line placement by matching the patch file against the diff hunk (@@ -47): the edits are only between the markers. abort_digest is empty, so I have no evidence the policy fired during verify (types 4/26). I did not open any fitness, WAL or output/ files. Separately, the claude.ai Notion MCP server needs authorization (via the claude.ai connector settings); it was not used for this review.\"}\n```","ttft_ms":6051,"type":"result","duration_ms":45479,"uuid":"bdb85f12-6f14-4e15-805b-d40b0a231bd3","ttft_stream_ms":1757,"time_to_request_ms":60,"first_content_frame_ms":1758,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
